\section{1. Introduction}
The CRISPR-Cas9 system is a molecular scissors capable of identifying and editing target sequences within the genome. Its primary application lies in the restoration of genetic diversity in crops, the correction of human genetic diseases, and the improvement of livestock efficiency. Despite its utility, the high complexity of the system necessitates rigorous structural analysis to ensure accuracy and safety.

\section{2. Principle of Action}
The CRISPR-Cas9 mechanism relies on the recognition of a specific DNA sequence, typically a guide RNA (gRNA), by the Cas9 endonuclease. Upon binding, the gRNA directs the Cas9 nuclease to cleave the target DNA between two specific nucleotides. The resulting double-strand break initiates the repair process.

\subsection{2.1 The Cascade of Events}
Upon cleavage, the Cas9 nuclease becomes a multi-subunit complex. The nuclease domain disassembles into the CAS9 (Cas9 Associated Protein 9), which interacts with the DNA duplex. The nuclease complex then moves to the 3' end of the cleaved strand. The passivating domain (p55) prevents premature extension, while the RuvC domain binds to the target site, forming a protospacer-adjacent motif.

\subsection{2.2 Processing by the TRF1 Clamp-and-Open Complex}
Following activation, the TRF1 clamp-and-open complex contacts the target DNA. The TRF1 domain binds the 5' end of the guide RNA, forming a physical lock. This complex subsequently recruits the 3' end of the guide RNA to catalyse the shear incision of the double-strand DNA. The resulting single-stranded DNA is then cleaved by the Cas9 endonuclease, creating a nick at the cleavage site.

